<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> to ski moguls, keep your upper body facing downhill, let your legs bend to absorb each bump, and turn on the top or side of the mogul rather than slamming into the trough. Plant your pole as you crest each bump to time the next turn, and start slow on a short, soft mogul run before trying steeper fields.</p></div>

<h2>What moguls are</h2>
<p>Moguls are the bumps that form when many skiers turn in the same places on a run. Over a few days of skiing, the snow gets pushed into mounds with troughs between them. Some runs are left ungroomed on purpose so moguls build up, and these are often marked as more difficult. Mogul skiing is also a freestyle competition event, but most people searching for it just want to get down a bumpy run with control.</p>
<p>Moguls are easier when the snow is soft, so a mild afternoon or a day after fresh snow is the best time to practice. Hard, icy bumps are much harder to absorb.</p>

<h2>Before you try moguls</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Be solid on blue runs.</strong> You should be able to link controlled turns and stop on demand. If you're still working on that, read <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %> first.</li>
  <li><strong>Know the trail rating.</strong> Mogul runs are often blue or black. See <%= link_to "ski trail ratings explained", guide_path("ski-trail-ratings") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a short, low-angle mogul run</strong> with an easy exit, so you can stop and reset.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to ski moguls, step by step</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Stand in an athletic stance.</strong> Feet hip-width apart, ankles and knees flexed, hands forward where you can see them. Leaning back is the most common mistake in bumps.</li>
  <li><strong>Face your upper body downhill.</strong> Your legs turn under you while your chest and shoulders stay pointed down the fall line. This keeps you balanced and ready for the next bump.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a line a few bumps ahead.</strong> Look ahead, not at your ski tips. Choosing your next two or three turns makes the rhythm smoother.</li>
  <li><strong>Turn on the top or shoulder of the mogul.</strong> As your skis reach the top of a bump, the tips and tails are briefly off the snow, which makes pivoting easy. Turn there instead of skiing straight into the trough.</li>
  <li><strong>Absorb with your legs.</strong> Let your knees come up toward your chest as you go over the bump, then extend your legs into the trough to keep your skis on the snow. Think of your legs as shock absorbers and your head as staying at a steady height.</li>
  <li><strong>Plant your pole to time the turn.</strong> Reach your downhill pole forward and plant it on the top or far side of the bump as you start each turn. The pole plant sets your rhythm.</li>
  <li><strong>Control speed with turn shape.</strong> Finish each turn across the hill enough to keep your speed in check. If you're going too fast, stop on the side of a bump and restart.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Choosing your line</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Around the outside:</strong> skiing the shoulders of the bumps is the slowest, most controlled line and the best place to start.</li>
  <li><strong>Through the troughs (zipper line):</strong> the fastest and most demanding line, used by competitive mogul skiers. Work up to it.</li>
  <li><strong>Over the tops:</strong> turning on the crest of each bump is a good middle ground for learning rhythm.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Drills to build skill</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Single bump turns:</strong> ski to one bump, turn on top of it, and stop. Repeat until it feels natural.</li>
  <li><strong>Side-slip the bumps:</strong> slide sideways down a short mogul section to get used to the terrain without speed.</li>
  <li><strong>Hop turns on groomers:</strong> quick, short turns on a smooth run build the leg speed you need in bumps.</li>
  <li><strong>Absorption drill:</strong> traverse slowly across a mogul field and practice pulling your knees up over each bump.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Gear and conditions</h2>
<p>Narrower, softer, slightly shorter skis are easier to pivot in bumps than wide, stiff skis. If your skis feel like they're fighting you, check the <%= link_to "ski size chart", ski_size_chart_path %>. Above all, soft snow makes moguls far more forgiving, so ski bumps after a warm spell or a fresh storm.</p>

<h2>Stay safe</h2>
<p>Ski in control and be able to stop at any point. Stop where people above can see you, not just below the crest of a bump, and look uphill before you start again. A lesson focused on bumps is one of the fastest ways to improve; see <%= link_to "ski lessons for adults", guide_path("ski-lessons-for-adults") %>.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "Are moguls hard to ski?", answer: "Moguls are harder than groomed runs because they demand quick turns, leg absorption, and good balance. Start on a short, soft mogul run once you're comfortable on blue groomers." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What is the easiest way to ski moguls?", answer: "Ski the outside shoulders of the bumps, turn on the top of each mogul where your skis can pivot easily, keep your chest facing downhill, and absorb each bump with your legs." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Why do I keep falling back in moguls?", answer: "Usually because you're leaning back or looking at your ski tips. Keep your hands forward, shins pressed into your boots, and eyes a few bumps ahead." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Are shorter skis better for moguls?", answer: "Shorter, narrower, softer skis are generally easier to turn quickly in moguls than long, wide, stiff skis." } %>
